{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CALIBRATION REPLAY (resumed session, 2026-08-21; bedrock via REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE; eval_solver.run_case against the live Pass-1 prompts).\n\nProtocol note (unrelated-fire fallback exercised, per the plan): the first replay found two post-fix cases firing — FP-failopen-waits-on-recorded-pid carried incidental defects (undefined SuiteTimeout, missing `import subprocess`; replaced with builtin TimeoutError + explicit import, liveness fix intact) and FP-failopen-grep-with-liveness-sentinel had a genuine liveness gap (the sentinel `[0-9]+ (passed|failed|error)` is satisfied by a zero-collection run; the seeded fix now requires `[1-9][0-9]*` — at least one test executed). Full 18-case re-run after revision:\n\nPRE-FIX (must fire): 9/9 fired, every quoted claim names the seeded fail-open defect —\n- R-failopen-absence-from-child-output (n=1): \"The test asserts absence of \"DeprecationWarning\" in the subprocess stderr with no liveness check on the producer, so it passes both when the worker runs cleanly AND when the worker crashes, is signal-killed, or emits nothing at all — a fail-open oracle that cannot distinguish its\"\n- R-failopen-rederived-convergence (n=1): \"test_comment_dedup_converges re-derives the dedup key with a copy of the production formula (hashlib.sha1('marker:'+body)) and asserts equality against it, instead of driving the real send/dedup path; it stays green when the live path breaks (convergence-twin fail-open oracle).\"\n- R-failopen-colorized-grep-oracle (n=2): \"run_kill_matrix.sh derives the surviving-mutation count from an absence grep (`grep -c \"^FAILED\" out.log`) with no liveness check on the pytest producer, so it reports 0 failures whether pytest passed, crashed, was killed, or emitted zero output — a fail-open oracle.\"\n- R-failopen-truncated-log-grep (n=1): \"The sweep pass/fail oracle is an absence-only grep (`! grep -q \"FAIL\" sweep.log`) over a background process's log with no producer-liveness check, so it reports \"sweep passed\" both when the sweep truly had no failures AND when the sweep was killed, never finished, or wrote a trun\"\n- R-failopen-unchecked-returncode (n=2): \"dc_pass_health ignores the subprocess return code, so a dc-check that crashed, was not found, or produced empty stdout returns True (healthy) because 'ERROR' is absent from empty output — the pass verdict is indistinguishable from the check never running.\"\n- R-failopen-silent-lookup-miss (n=1): \"The added lookup of each variable in KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS silently skips (`continue`) on a miss, so an unknown/unregistered helper produces neither a row nor an error — the miss is invisible and the generated registry cannot distinguish 'all helpers known' from 'some variables silen\"\n- R-failopen-environ-scan-skip (n=1): \"The membership test `if name not in KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS: continue` silently drops any os.environ read whose variable name is not already in KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS. A genuinely new/unknown env-var read is skipped with no error, so it never appears in the generated registry and the miss i\"\n- R-failopen-pgrep-self-match-monitor (n=2): \"`pgrep -f sys.argv[1]` matches the monitor process's own argv, so the wait loop cannot distinguish 'suite still running' from 'only the monitor itself matches the pattern'; a finished suite still looks live and the loop spins until timeout.\"\n- R-failopen-pgrep-self-match-waiter (n=1): \"`pgrep -f \"$1\"` matches the waiter's own shell process, whose command line contains the search pattern (`$1`), so the `while` loop never observes the target process exit — a finished review reports 'running' until timeout. The check cannot distinguish a live target from a self-ma\"\n\nPOST-FIX (must be silent): 9/9 zero-finding —\n  FP-failopen-absence-with-liveness-anchor n=0, FP-failopen-drives-real-send-path n=0, FP-failopen-grep-with-liveness-sentinel n=0, FP-failopen-completion-sentinel-first n=0, FP-failopen-returncode-checked n=0, FP-failopen-lookup-miss-fails-closed n=0, FP-failopen-environ-scan-fails-closed n=0, FP-failopen-waits-on-recorded-pid n=0, FP-failopen-waiter-excludes-self n=0\n\nFP POSTURE: both hosts already blocking (tests @0.54, error-handling @0.50), so no advisory ramp. The 9 post-fix no-fire cases are all silent. Standing no-fire families replayed in the same session: 9/9 code-review-tests FP-* silent; code-review-base 5/6 silent — FP-sourced-bash fired findings about an unchecked `source` return status. Controlled A/B (3 runs each on the modified vs the HEAD baseline prompt): baseline fires the SAME claims 2/3 runs, modified 1/3 — pre-existing borderline stochastic behavior of that seeded diff, not a regression from the fail-open language. No escalation to a new criterion is needed: the language fires reliably from both hosts (9/9 recall), so the operator ruling's placement stands."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787358195642885001,"uuid":"6b6fd9e2-ff86-4592-8b13-3e42791e3f01"}